    "body": "Proficient Auto Logistics, North America’s largest finished vehicle logistics platform, sees better days ahead, but its 2026 third quarter signaled a still-difficult operating environment for car haulers. The Jacksonville, Florida-based company pointed to several levers—higher pricing, improved fuel surcharge recovery and better-than-expected acquisition synergies—to repair margins.\nProficient ( NASDAQ: PAL ) reported a headline net loss of $10 million for the period. Excluding acquisition-related and other nonrecurring items, it recorded a modest operating profit. A 99.8% adjusted operating ratio (inverse of operating margin ) was 350 basis points worse year over year.\nShares of PAL were down 20% in early Friday trading. The stock has fallen more than 70% this year.\nTable: Proficient’s key performance indicators\nRevenue increased 30% y/y to $149 million. The quarter benefitted from the August acquisition of California-based Hansen & Adkins . The roughly six-week revenue contribution added $51 million to the quarter. However, third-quarter revenue from Proficient’s legacy network was down 16% y/y.\n(The acquisition followed recent M&A in the space and the 2025 shutdown of Jack Cooper.)\nTotal vehicle deliveries increased 39% y/y with revenue per delivery (ex-fuel) down 10% y/y. The revenue mix was slightly less favorable and the addition of Hansen & Adkins lowered Proficient’s overall length of haul.\nMost of the company’s contracts renew every three years and it is targeting “at least” mid-single-digit rate increases. It said rate increases averaged a little over 10% on the $25 million in contracts renewed since Sept. 1.\nIt’s attempting to renegotiate below-market contracts ahead of expiration, but warned it could potentially lose this business. It is also increasing spot exposure as only 7% to 12% of the volume currently moving through the network is priced above contract rates.\n“Fuel surcharge under-recovery” produced near-breakeven results on the operating line.\nProficient’s OEM customers set the fuel surcharge tables, which are slow to adjust. (Diesel prices increased in nine out of 13 weeks in the third quarter, finishing the period 48% higher y/y.) Proficient said the fuel headwinds negatively impacted the OR by 200 to 250 bps.\nThe company also called out driver wages, recruiting and retention costs as detractors. Further, insurance and claims expenses were up 140 bps y/y as a percentage of revenue.\nProficient forecast fourth-quarter revenue of $180 million to $195 million, which was below the consensus estimate of $203 million at the time of the print. It expects to record 50 to 100 bps of sequential margin improvement in the period, roughly implying a 99% OR.\nIt said car inventories available for shipment near the ports are up 15%, and that it has added more than 200 new third-party carriers in the past 30 days. The Hansen & Adkins capacity integration has led to better asset utilization and more than 900 incremental units hauled per day (over $500,000 in monthly revenue).\nIt forecast a full-year 2027 OR of 95% to 97%, with further improvement occurring in 2028 (92% to 94%). Better pricing and fuel surcharge recovery, along with synergies from the acquisition, are the catalysts. Acquisition synergies are now expected to total $20 million to $30 million, well above the initial guide of $6 million to $10 million.\nLonger term, it sees a path to a sub-90% OR.\nProficient will use cash to fund equipment replacement capex ($20 million in 2027), to pay down debt (net debt of $201 million) and to buy back stock ($13.9 million remaining on an open authorization in relation to its $73 million market cap).\nNet debt leverage was approximately 4x at the end of the quarter, but is forecast to be below 3x by the end of the 2027 third quarter, as full-year EBITDA is expected to double to $60 million to $70 million.\nWhy it matters? There is only one publicly traded auto hauler. Proficient’s quarterly results provide a rare look at the transportation side of the automotive industry. Recent acquisitions highlight a major consolidation across the space, reshaping capacity and competitive dynamics.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Todd Maiden:\nForward Air pauses sale of intermodal unit\nHigher shipment weights help ArcBest beat Q3 expectations\nPeak season inventory surge bolsters TL carriers’ double-digit rate ask",
